.TH IO_ADDBUF_S 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
iob_adds \- Add a string to an I/O batch buffer

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/iob.h>

.IB int
.IB iob_adds(
.IB io_batch* b,
.IB const char* s
.IB );

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBiob_adds\fR function adds a string to an I/O batch buffer. This function is a convenience wrapper around the \fBiob_addbuf\fR function, which adds a buffer of data to the batch.

The function takes the following parameters:

.IB b
Pointer to the I/O batch buffer.

.IB s
Pointer to the string to be added.

The function returns 0 on success, or a negative value indicating an error.

The function is part of the \fBiob\fR library, which provides a framework for managing I/O operations in batch mode.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBiob_adds\fR function to add a string to an I/O batch buffer:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/iob.h>

int main() {
  io_batch batch;
  io_batch_init(&batch);

  if (iob_adds(&batch, "Hello, World!") != 0) {
    perror("iob_adds");
    return 1;
  }

  if (iob_execute(&batch) != 0) {
    perror("iob_execute");
    return 1;
  }

  printf("String added to batch buffer\n");

  return 0;
}
.nef